 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The number of steps depends on the distance to the surface and the local curvature. In empty space, steps are large, leading to $O(1)$ or $O(\log D)$ behavior relative to distance $D$. Near the surface, steps become small, leading to $O(1/\epsilon)$ behavior. Worst-case is grazing angles where steps are small but the ray travels far [S5].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per ray, as only the current position and distance are stored. The SDF function itself may require memory if it is a texture or a complex neural network [S2][S7, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Performance varies by scene complexity. In tests with simple shapes, Sphere Tracing (ST) was comparable to Grid Sphere Tracing (GST) but slower than analytic methods for simple primitives [S1, unofficial]. Bounding Sphere Optimization can increase FPS by 25% with 10 enclosed objects [S37, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** No preprocessing required for simple analytic SDFs. Perfectly smooth geometry. Easy to combine shapes using CSG operations (min, max, smooth min) [S19, S21, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Slow for complex scenes with many primitives if the SDF evaluation is expensive. Oscillation near surfaces. Requires Lipschitz continuity. Not suitable for non-distance fields without modification [S2, S5].
-*   **When to Use:** Real-time rendering of procedural geometry, implicit surfaces, and smooth deformations. When memory bandwidth is limited and geometry is complex but defined by simple functions.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When the scene is composed of large, static polygonal meshes (use rasterization or ray tracing). When the SDF is not available or expensive to evaluate (e.g., complex mesh-based SDFs without acceleration structures).
+*   **Time Complexity:** The number of iterations depends on the distance to the surface and the local geometry. In empty space, steps are large, leading to $O(1)$ or very few iterations. Near the surface, steps become small, leading to $O(\log(1/\epsilon))$ iterations for convergence [S2][S31, unofficial]. Worst-case scenarios occur when rays graze surfaces or pass close to parallel surfaces, requiring many small steps [S5].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory for the core algorithm. Memory usage depends on the storage of the SDF (implicit function evaluation vs. voxel grid) and any acceleration structures [S37, unofficial][S43, abstract only, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Performance varies significantly by scene complexity. In simple scenes with few primitives, sphere tracing is very fast. In complex scenes with many primitives, the cost of evaluating the SDF for each step becomes dominant. Bounding sphere optimizations can increase FPS by 25% when >3 objects are enclosed [S37, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** No preprocessing required for implicit surfaces; perfect geometry representation; easy to combine primitives using CSG operations; adaptive step size reduces evaluations in empty space [S17, S31, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires the scene to be representable as an SDF or Lipschitz function; can be slow for complex scenes with many primitives if no acceleration structure is used; sensitive to the Lipschitz constant; may require many iterations near flat or grazing surfaces [S2, S5][S31, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Ideal for rendering procedural geometry, fractals, and smooth CSG combinations where explicit mesh generation is undesirable or too costly [S17, S31, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for scenes with explicit polygonal meshes unless converted to SDFs; may be inefficient for highly detailed, non-procedural geometry without acceleration structures [S2][S37, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Sphere Tracing was introduced by John Hart in 1996 in the paper "Sphere Tracing: A Geometric Method for the Antialiased Ray Tracing of Implicit Surfaces" [S14, unofficial][S40]. It is based on the geometric property that a sphere centered at a point on the ray with radius equal to the distance to the nearest surface cannot contain any part of the surface. This allows the ray to safely advance by that distance. The method is a specific case of ray marching where the step size is derived from the Lipschitz constant of the implicit function [S2]. It has become a standard technique in real-time graphics for rendering implicit surfaces, particularly in shaders [S24, S30, unofficial].
+Sphere Tracing was introduced by John Hart in 1996 in the paper "Sphere Tracing: A Geometric Method for the Antialiased Ray Tracing of Implicit Surfaces" [S14, unofficial][S40]. It was developed as a method to render implicit surfaces defined by distance functions without the need for explicit polygonal meshes or complex intersection algorithms [S14, unofficial][S40]. The method leverages the geometric property of distance functions to provide safe stepping distances, ensuring accurate and antialiased rendering of complex implicit shapes [S14, unofficial][S40]. It has since become a fundamental technique in procedural rendering, real-time graphics, and neural rendering [S17, S31, unofficial]. Variants and optimizations, such as relaxed sphere tracing and enhanced sphere tracing, have been developed to address performance bottlenecks in specific scenarios [S1, unofficial][S5][S39, unofficial].
 
